{"manifest":{"slug":"alabama-mental-health-systems","title":"Alabama Mental Health Systems Report","short_title":"Alabama Mental Health","subtitle":"A descriptive analysis of Alabama's mental health system of systems, for advocates, planners and coalition partners","question":"What does Alabama's mental health system of systems look like in October 2026 (who decides, who pays, what exists, who delivers it, who is reached and who is missed), and how well is each statement about it supported by its source?","as_of":"2026-10-03","authors":["Little Orange Fish"],"venue":"DRAFT FOR REVIEW. Not approved by Little Orange Fish and not peer reviewed. Report by Little Orange Fish; source check, change log, maps and primers prepared with Claude.","year":2026,"report":{"publisher":"Little Orange Fish, Huntsville, Alabama","role":"backbone organization for the North Alabama Mental Health Coalition","version":"v261003.1","based_on":"Version 2 draft of 3 October 2026 (69-page PDF)","supersedes":"v250603 (June 2025)","status":"draft-for-review","intended_home":"NorthAlabamaMentalHealthCoalition.org, on approval"},"plain_abstract":"Alabama's mental health system is not one system. A state agency and its contracted community centers, Medicaid and private insurers, hospitals, schools, courts and jails, housing coalitions and hundreds of nonprofits together decide whether a person can find help, afford it, reach it and keep it. This report by Little Orange Fish describes all of them in one place: eight domains adapted from the World Health Organization's health-system building blocks, and four sections on the systems outside the Department of Mental Health that shape outcomes. This site presents the October 2026 draft after every claim in it was checked against its cited source. You can read the report with each sentence's source one hover away, see what the check changed and what it could not settle, explore how the domains depend on one another, and compare Alabama's counties with on